# What prettier may not touch, and why.
#
# This file is load-bearing now that `prettier --check` is a CI gate: anything
# not listed here is rewritten by `prettier --write .`, and several of the paths
# below were silently corrupted by exactly that while this change was written.

/coverage
/.nx/
/.claude/coverage/

# Generated route types. `react-router typegen` writes these on every
# `nx typecheck`, so without this the check passes on a fresh clone and fails
# for anyone who has typechecked - a gate whose answer depends on what you ran
# last is worse than no gate. Prettier does not read `.gitignore`, so being
# untracked is not enough to keep them out.
/apps/vectreal-platform/.react-router/

# Lockfiles. Their format belongs to the tool that writes them, and reformatting
# one is a 13,000-line diff that pnpm did not ask for.
pnpm-lock.yaml
skills-lock.json

# Vendored third-party builds. `draco_decoder.js` is a minified Emscripten
# bundle; prettier expands it to 50,000 lines and it is not ours to style.
/apps/vectreal-platform/public/

# Drizzle output. The SQL and the `meta/*_snapshot.json` files are generated by
# `nx run vectreal-platform:drizzle-generate` and read back by it - the
# snapshots are how the next migration knows the current schema. Reformatting
# them desynchronizes the meta from the migrations it describes, which is the
# one thing the migration rules say never to do by hand.
/apps/vectreal-platform/supabase/migrations/

# Operational definitions, which a formatter has no business touching.
#
# Not a style preference. `prettier --write .` reformatted
# `cd-platform-production.yaml` and `cd-platform-staging.yaml` while this change
# was being written - two lines of quote style, in the files that deploy
# production. The same argument covers the agent hooks and editor settings:
# infrastructure is edited deliberately, and no feature branch should reformat
# it as a side effect.
/.github/
/.agents/
/.claude/
/.vscode/

# Prose is authored, not generated.
#
# Docs and newsroom articles are the product's writing, and their line breaks
# are a decision someone made. Prettier normalizes tables, list markers and
# fence indentation: a diff across 42 content files that renders identically.
#
# Listed here rather than merely left unformatted, because an ignored path is
# one `prettier --write .` cannot touch by accident - which is the failure this
# whole change exists to remove.
*.md
*.mdx
